==== A. "On a clock of 12 hours there are five such ways" ====
numbers of steps to a full turn on 12 hours (divisors of 12 but 1): [2, 3, 4, 6, 12]

==== B. "12 alone" on 12 hours: 2 + 3 = 5, "a pair and a triangle" ====
markings of 1 to 5 marks that balance at 12 alone: 24 ; their sizes: [5]
of them, a pair of opposite marks with a triangle of evenly spaced marks: 24 of 24
the page's marking: ((0, 6), (1, 5, 9))
the first formula for "12 alone" (no power of 2 in the pattern, e = 0; one short power, 4, k = 1): 2^0 * (2^1 + 3) = 5

==== C. The clock of 6 hours: "five marks that balance at 6 and at no other divisor always mark one hour twice" ====
markings of 1 to 7 marks on 6 hours that balance at 6 and at no other divisor, by number of marks: {5: 6, 7: 12}
least number of marks: 5
the five-mark ones, by how often each marked hour is marked: {(2, 1, 1, 1): 6}
every one of them marks exactly one hour twice and no hour more often: True
every one of them is a pair of opposite marks with a triangle: True
markings of any number of marks at different hours on 6 hours that balance at 6 and at no other divisor: 0

==== D. The clock of 18 hours: "the marks 0, 1, 2, 4 and 10 do it" ====
0, 1, 2, 4, 10 on 18 hours balance exactly at: (6,) ; all at different hours: True
markings of 1, 2, 3, 4 marks on 18 hours (repeats allowed) that balance at 6 and at no other divisor of 18: [0, 0, 0, 0]
wound back onto 6 hours the five marks are [0, 1, 2, 4, 4] with pattern (6,)
18 = 6 * 3: the factor is 3 ; the least number is 5; so the factor is not greater than the least number (the full account says so)

==== E. The clock of 36 hours: "12, 18 and 36" is a pattern in which every multiple of a chosen divisor is chosen too ====
"12, 18 and 36": True ;  "12 alone": False  (36 is a multiple of 12 and is missing)
largest divisors of 36 not in the pattern: [4, 6, 9] ; rings of [9, 6, 4] marks; together 19 (the full account says 9, 6 and 4: 19)
the five marks on 36 hours balance exactly at: (12,)

==== F. "Why ask?": two sets tile the 12-hour clock exactly when their sizes multiply to 12 and at every divisor but 1 one of them balances ====
pairs of sets whose sizes multiply to 12: 339792; of them tilings: 2328; the two tests agree on: 339792
the pair 0 and 6 with the turns 0..5: True ; 0 and 6 balance at [4, 12] ; the turns 0..5 balance at [2, 3, 6]
